---

<Lecture
  chapterSlug="07-file-io"
  lessonSlug="02-write-log"
  hook="Mode 'a' = append. Mode 'w' = erase. Pick the wrong one and your log loses history every call."
>
  <CodePlayground
    autoRun
    code={`def append_log(path, message):
    with open(path, "a") as f:
        f.write(message + "\\n")

def read_log(path):
    with open(path) as f:
        return [line.rstrip("\\n") for line in f]

# Three calls — all three messages survive.
append_log("audit.log", "user logged in")
append_log("audit.log", "user clicked submit")
append_log("audit.log", "user logged out")

for line in read_log("audit.log"):
    print(line)`}
  />

  <Callout tone="pitfall" title="The 'w' trap — try it">
    <p>
      Change <code>"a"</code> to <code>"w"</code> in <code>append_log</code>
      and re-run. Only the LAST message survives — every call erases the
      previous content. That's the entire reason mode <code>"a"</code> exists.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>From ATBS ch.9</h2>
  <Callout tone="note" title="On mode 'w'">
    <p>
      <em>"When you call open() with the 'w' mode, the file's contents will
      be erased."</em> If you want to add content to an existing file, you
      have to use the 'a' append mode.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</Lecture>
